    "long_instructions": "## Investigate & Tinker\r\n\r\nTwo playlist apps play the same five songs. App A uses a `for` loop. App B uses a `while` loop.\r\n\r\nDo This:\r\n\r\n1. Trace both apps by hand using the table in your Unit Guide. For each pass, write the value of the counter and the running total.\r\n2. Predict: will the two totals match? Will the two counters match?\r\n3. Run the program and compare it with your trace. What matches? What doesn't?\r\n4. Tinker: change App B's condition from `song < 5` to `song <= 5`. Predict first: do the totals still match?\r\n5. Change one thing in App A so the totals match again. Do the counters match now?",
    "start_sources": {
      "files": {
        "0": {
          "id": "0",
          "name": "main.py",
          "contents": "# App A: for loop\ntotal_a = 0\nfor j in range(5):\n    total_a = total_a + (3 + j)\nprint(\"App A total: \" + str(total_a) + \" min\")\nprint(\"App A counter: \" + str(j))\n\n# App B: while loop\ntotal_b = 0\nsong = 0\nwhile song < 5:\n    total_b = total_b + (3 + song)\n    song = song + 1\nprint(\"App B total: \" + str(total_b) + \" min\")\nprint(\"App B counter: \" + str(song))",
          "folderId": "0",
          "active": true
        }
      },
      "folders": {
      },
      "openFiles": [
        "0"
      ]
    },
    "teacher_markdown": "- Expected output before tinkering:\r\n\r\n```\r\nApp A total: 25 min\r\nApp A counter: 4\r\nApp B total: 25 min\r\nApp B counter: 5\r\n```\r\n\r\n- The totals match but the counters do not. Same output, different side effect: after the `for` loop `j` is `4`, and after the `while` loop `song` is `5`.\r\n- After step 4 (`song <= 5`), App B prints `total: 33 min` and `counter: 6`. App B now plays six songs, so its total no longer matches App A (`25`).\r\n- Step 5: any change that makes the totals match is acceptable. The most common is changing App A to `range(6)`, which gives:\r\n\r\n```\r\nApp A total: 33 min\r\nApp A counter: 5\r\n```\r\n\r\n- Reverting App B also works. Either way the counters still differ by 1, because the `for` loop stops at `n - 1` and the `while` loop's counter ends at `n`.\r\n- Discussion goal: students should recognize that two programs can produce the same output and still leave different values behind, and that matching totals do not mean the programs are the same.\r\n- Watch for: students who say the programs are \"the same\" because the totals match. Ask: what is different about the variable after the loop?\r\n- Students who change the `3 + j` formula to fix the total are changing the songs, not the loop. Ask what the two apps are supposed to play.",
